{%- comment -%}
  Pinned feature scroll — the page holds still while the phone turns.

  The behaviour Henry asked for, and the one noth.in uses: as you scroll, the
  section locks to the viewport, the device rotates through a sequence of
  positions, each screen replaces the last, and a callout arrow points at the
  feature being shown.

  **Why this is CSS and not a rendered video.** A video cannot respond to scroll
  position — it can only be scrubbed, which means downloading the whole file
  first and decoding it on a timer that has nothing to do with the user's
  thumb. It is also fixed resolution, weighs megabytes on a page we just cut
  3 MB from, and every copy change means a re-render. Here the phone is a real
  element: crisp at any density, effectively free, and edited by changing a
  number. Video tools earn their place making video — ads, the demo reel — not
  making a page behave.

  ── How the pinning works ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The outer element is tall (one viewport per scene). The stage inside it is
  `position: sticky`, so it parks under the nav while that tall parent scrolls
  past. That parent also carries `view-timeline-name: --pin`, which descendants
  read — so every animation below is driven by scroll position rather than by
  time, and runs on the compositor instead of the main thread.

  Each scene owns a slice of the track via `animation-range`, computed in Liquid
  so adding a fifth block re-divides the timeline automatically.

  ── Degrading ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Everything scroll-driven lives inside `@supports (animation-timeline: view())`.
  Without it — and under `prefers-reduced-motion` — the section becomes a plain
  vertical list of screens with their captions: no pinning, no rotation, all the
  same information. Content never depends on an animation having run.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign count = section.blocks.size -%}
{%- if count == 0 -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- assign slice = 100 | divided_by: count -%}

<section
  class="pin full-width{% if section.settings.tone == 'dark' %} pin--dark{% endif %}"
  style="--pin-scenes: {{ count }};"
>
  <div class="pin-intro">
    {%- if section.settings.label != blank -%}
      <p class="pin-label">{{ section.settings.label }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
    <h2 class="pin-title">{{ section.settings.title }}</h2>
    {%- if section.settings.intro != blank -%}
      <div class="pin-copy">{{ section.settings.intro }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="pin-track">
    <div class="pin-stage">
      <div class="pin-device">
        <div class="pin-frame">
          <div class="pin-notch" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="pin-screen">
            <img
              class="pin-idle-screen"
              src="{{ 'clippar-app-splash-logo.jpg' | asset_img_url: '720x' }}"
              srcset="
                {{ 'clippar-app-splash-logo.jpg' | asset_img_url: '640x' }} 295w,
                {{ 'clippar-app-splash-logo.jpg' | asset_img_url: '720x' }} 640w,
                {{ 'clippar-app-splash-logo.jpg' | asset_url }} 1082w
              "
              alt=""
              width="1082"
              height="2344"
              decoding="async"
              sizes="(min-width: 900px) 17rem, (max-height: 720px) 58vw, 65vw"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
            {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
              {%- assign from = forloop.index0 | times: slice -%}
              {%- assign to = forloop.index | times: slice -%}
              {%- assign high_res_pin_asset = false -%}
              {%- if block.settings.asset_name contains 'live-recording-mounted-phone' or block.settings.asset_name contains 'shot-detection-pose-trim' -%}
                {%- assign high_res_pin_asset = true -%}
              {%- endif -%}
              <img
                class="pin-shot pin-shot--{{ forloop.index }}"
                style="--from: {{ from }}%; --to: {{ to }}%;"
                {% if block.settings.asset_name != blank %}
                  {% if high_res_pin_asset %}
                    src="{{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '1200x' }}"
                    srcset="
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '720x' }} 332w,
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '1200x' }} 554w,
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '1600x' }} 739w,
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_url }} 1179w
                    "
                  {% else %}
                    src="{{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '720x' }}"
                    srcset="
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '640x' }} 295w,
                      {{ block.settings.asset_name | asset_img_url: '720x' }} 640w
                    "
                  {% endif %}
                {% elsif block.settings.image != blank %}
                  src="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 640 }}"
                  srcset="
                    {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w,
                    {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 960 }} 960w,
                    {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 1280 }} 1280w
                  "
                {% endif %}
                alt="{{ block.settings.alt | default: block.settings.caption | escape }}"
                width="1179"
                height="2556"
                decoding="async"
                sizes="(min-width: 900px) 17rem, (max-height: 720px) 58vw, 65vw"
                {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
              >
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Callouts sit beside the phone, not on it — text over a moving screenshot
        has uncontrolled contrast. The arrow is a CSS rule with a rotated head,
        so it scales and recolours with the rest of the type.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="pin-callouts">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- assign from = forloop.index0 | times: slice -%}
          {%- assign to = forloop.index | times: slice -%}
          <figure
            class="pin-callout pin-callout--{{ forloop.index }}"
            style="--from: {{ from }}%; --to: {{ to }}%;"
          >
            <span class="pin-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <figcaption>
              <span class="pin-step">{{ forloop.index | prepend: '0' | slice: -2, 2 }} / {{ count | prepend: '0' | slice: -2, 2 }}</span>
              {%- comment -%}
                A real <h3>, not a styled span. The page runs h1 (hero) → h2
                (this section) → h3 (each feature), which is the structure
                Google reads to work out what the page is about — and these five
                headings are the only place the page says "shot detection" or
                "filming your round" in a position that carries any weight.

                All five are in the DOM regardless of which is currently
                visible, so all five are indexed; the opacity is presentation.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <h3 class="pin-feature">{{ block.settings.feature }}</h3>
              <span class="pin-caption">{{ block.settings.caption }}</span>
              {%- comment -%}
                The sub-features for this section of the app, straight from
                Henry's own workbook. One per line in the setting; blank lines
                are dropped so trailing newlines cannot render an empty bullet.

                Split on a literal newline, captured into a variable, rather
                than the usual `newline_to_br | split: '<br />'` trick — that
                depends on the exact tag Shopify emits, and it silently produced
                zero items here rather than failing loudly.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- capture nl %}
{% endcapture -%}
              {%- assign points = block.settings.points | split: nl -%}
              {%- if points.size > 0 -%}
                <ul class="pin-points">
                  {%- for point in points -%}
                    {%- assign line = point | strip_newlines | strip -%}
                    {%- if line != blank -%}
                      <li>{{ line }}</li>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </ul>
              {%- endif -%}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% stylesheet %}
  .pin {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--content-grid);
    background: var(--bg);
    padding-block: clamp(64px, 9vw, 104px) 0;
    overflow: clip;
  }

  .pin--dark { background: var(--bg2); }

  .pin-intro { grid-column: 2; max-width: 46rem; }
  .pin-track { grid-column: 2; }

  .pin-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--green);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .pin-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--white);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* One word in the accent colour so the heading has a focal point rather than
     being a uniform slab of caps. */
  .pin-title em { font-style: normal; color: var(--green); }

  .pin-copy { color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; margin-block-start: 1rem; }
  .pin-copy p { margin: 0; }

  /* --- fallback layout: a readable stack ------------------------------------
     This is what renders without scroll-driven animation support, and it has to
     be complete on its own. Everything below is progressive enhancement. */
  .pin-stage { display: grid; gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem); padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem); }
  .pin-device { display: grid; place-items: center; }

  .pin-frame {
    position: relative;
    width: min(64vw, 15rem);
    border-radius: 2rem;
    padding: 0.5rem;
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, #2f342f 0%, #12160f 55%, #262b2c 100%);
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.16) inset,
      0 40px 80px -28px rgba(0,0,0,0.9),
      0 10px 24px -10px rgba(0,0,0,0.65);
  }

  .pin-notch {
    position: absolute; top: 0.5rem; left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%);
    width: 32%; height: 0.85rem; background: #0a0d08;
    border-radius: 0 0 0.6rem 0.6rem; z-index: 2;
  }

  .pin-screen {
    position: relative;
    border-radius: 1.6rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
    aspect-ratio: 886 / 1920;
  }

  .pin-idle-screen,
  .pin-shot {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .pin-idle-screen {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  .pin-shot {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: block;
  }

  .pin-callouts { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; }

  .pin-callout { margin: 0; display: flex; gap: 0.85rem; align-items: flex-start; }

  .pin-callout figcaption { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; }

  .pin-step {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    color: var(--green);
  }

  /* The feature name is the headline of its scene, so it is set like one.
     Display face, big, tight — it has to hold its own next to a phone. */
  .pin-feature {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--white);
    margin: 0.1rem 0 0.4rem;
  }

  .pin-caption {
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.5vw, 1.15rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--white);
    max-width: 24rem;
  }

  /* The sub-features for this section of the app. Ticked rather than bulleted —
     a bullet reads as prose, a tick reads as a capability. */
  .pin-points {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0.55rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    max-width: 24rem;
  }

  .pin-points li {
    position: relative;
    padding-inline-start: 1.05rem;
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .pin-points li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    top: 0.42em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.22rem;
    border-left: 1.5px solid var(--green);
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--green);
    transform: rotate(-45deg);
  }

  /* The arrow: a rule with a rotated head. Hidden in the stacked fallback,
     where there is nothing to point at. */
  .pin-arrow { display: none; }

  /* --- enhanced: pinned, rotating, one scene at a time --------------------- */
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .pin-track {
        /* One viewport of scroll per scene, plus one to read the last. */
        height: calc(100svh * (var(--pin-scenes) + 1));
        view-timeline-name: --pin;
        view-timeline-axis: block;
      }

      .pin-stage {
        position: sticky;
        top: var(--nav-h, 83px);
        height: calc(100svh - var(--nav-h, 83px));
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        align-items: center;
        gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5rem);
        padding-block: 0;
      }

      /* Shared perspective so the device reads as an object in a room. */
      .pin-device { perspective: 1400px; perspective-origin: 50% 45%; }

      .pin-frame {
        width: min(30vw, 17rem);
        animation: pin-turn linear both;
        animation-timeline: --pin;
        animation-range: contain 0% contain 100%;
        transform-style: preserve-3d;
      }

      .pin-idle-screen {
        animation: pin-idle-screen linear both;
        animation-timeline: --pin;
        animation-range: contain 0% contain 100%;
      }

      @keyframes pin-idle-screen {
        0%   { opacity: 1; }
        2%   { opacity: 1; }
        3%   { opacity: 0; }
        97%  { opacity: 0; }
        98%  { opacity: 1; }
        100% { opacity: 1; }
      }

      /* The device travels through positions rather than simply spinning —
         yaw, a little pitch, and a drift across the stage, so each scene is
         framed slightly differently. */
      @keyframes pin-turn {
        0%   { transform: rotateY(28deg)  rotateX(6deg)  translate3d(6%, 4%, 0)  scale(0.94); }
        35%  { transform: rotateY(-6deg)  rotateX(-2deg) translate3d(-2%, -2%, 0) scale(1); }
        70%  { transform: rotateY(-22deg) rotateX(4deg)  translate3d(3%, 2%, 0)  scale(0.97); }
        100% { transform: rotateY(4deg)   rotateX(-5deg) translate3d(-4%, -3%, 0) scale(1.02); }
      }

      /* Screens cross-fade within their own slice of the track. Each carries
         its own --from/--to, written by Liquid, so the slices always divide the
         timeline evenly however many blocks exist. */
      .pin-shot {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        opacity: 0;
        animation: pin-swap linear both;
        animation-timeline: --pin;
        animation-range: contain var(--from) contain var(--to);
      }

      /* The first screen is visible WITHOUT the animation having run.
         A scroll timeline can fail to resolve — a browser that supports the
         syntax but cannot attach the timeline leaves every animation inactive,
         and with a base of `opacity: 0` that renders an empty phone with no
         sign anything is wrong. Observed exactly that. When the timeline does
         resolve the animation overrides this, so it costs nothing. */
      .pin-shot--1 { opacity: 1; }

      /* Stop-motion, not cross-fade.
         `steps(1, end)` on the opacity means a screen is either on or off —
         it snaps to the next one the way a stop-motion frame changes, rather
         than the two ghosting through each other. Two screenshots dissolving
         into one another is mush; a cut is legible.
         The scale still eases, so the phone keeps moving through the cut. */
      @keyframes pin-swap {
        0%   { opacity: 0; }
        6%   { opacity: 1; }
        94%  { opacity: 1; }
        100% { opacity: 0; }
      }

      .pin-shot { animation-timing-function: steps(1, end); }

      .pin-callouts { position: relative; }

      .pin-callout {
        position: absolute;
        inset-block-start: 50%;
        translate: 0 -50%;
        opacity: 0;
        animation: pin-callout-in linear both;
        animation-timeline: --pin;
        animation-range: contain var(--from) contain var(--to);
      }

      /* Same reasoning as .pin-shot--1: the first callout must be readable
         even if no animation ever runs, so the section is never a phone with
         nothing beside it. */
      .pin-callout--1 { opacity: 1; }

      @keyframes pin-callout-in {
        0%   { opacity: 0; translate: 1.5rem -50%; }
        18%  { opacity: 1; translate: 0 -50%; }
        82%  { opacity: 1; translate: 0 -50%; }
        100% { opacity: 0; translate: -1rem -50%; }
      }

      /* Arrow pointing back at the device. */
      .pin-arrow {
        display: block;
        position: relative;
        flex: none;
        width: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
        height: 1px;
        margin-block-start: 1.6rem;
        background: var(--green);
      }

      .pin-arrow::before {
        content: '';
        position: absolute;
        inset-inline-start: 0;
        top: 50%;
        width: 0.5rem;
        height: 0.5rem;
        border-left: 1px solid var(--green);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--green);
        transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(45deg);
      }

      @media (max-width: 599px) {
        .pin-stage {
          grid-template-columns: 1fr;
          grid-template-rows: auto auto;
          align-content: start;
          gap: clamp(0.75rem, 1.8svh, 1.05rem);
          padding-block: clamp(0.65rem, 1.7svh, 1rem) clamp(1rem, 2.4svh, 1.35rem);
        }

        .pin-device {
          align-self: center;
          perspective: 1000px;
          perspective-origin: 50% 45%;
        }

        /* Smaller than it was, on purpose. The bullets below are the
           substance of each scene and they now render on mobile too, so the
           device gives up the vertical space they need. */
        .pin-frame {
          width: min(56vw, 14rem);
          animation-name: pin-mobile-turn;
        }

        @keyframes pin-mobile-turn {
          0%   { transform: rotateY(14deg)  rotateX(4deg)  translate3d(2%, 3%, 0) scale(0.96); }
          35%  { transform: rotateY(-4deg)  rotateX(-1deg) translate3d(-1%, 2%, 0) scale(1); }
          70%  { transform: rotateY(-12deg) rotateX(3deg)  translate3d(1%, 2%, 0) scale(0.98); }
          100% { transform: rotateY(3deg)   rotateX(-3deg) translate3d(-1%, 2%, 0) scale(1); }
        }

        .pin-callouts {
          align-self: start;
          min-height: 12.6rem;
          width: 100%;
        }

        .pin-callout {
          inset: 0 0 auto;
          display: block;
          translate: 0 0;
          animation-name: pin-mobile-callout-in;
        }

        @keyframes pin-mobile-callout-in {
          0%   { opacity: 0; translate: 0 0.65rem; }
          18%  { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
          82%  { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
          100% { opacity: 0; translate: 0 -0.55rem; }
        }

        .pin-callout figcaption {
          max-width: 24rem;
          margin-inline: auto;
          gap: 0.22rem;
        }

        .pin-arrow { display: none; }

        .pin-step {
          font-size: 0.64rem;
          letter-spacing: 0.16em;
        }

        .pin-feature {
          font-size: 1.85rem;
          line-height: 0.94;
          margin-block: 0.05rem 0.25rem;
        }

        .pin-caption {
          display: block;
          font-size: 0.88rem;
          line-height: 1.35;
          max-width: 100%;
        }

        /* Henry asked for the desktop bullet list on mobile too. It was
           hidden purely to save vertical space; the space now comes out of
           the device instead, which is the right trade — the bullets are the
           only place the page states what each scene actually does. */
        .pin-points {
          display: grid;
          font-size: 0.72rem;
          gap: 0.2rem;
          margin-block-start: 0.4rem;
        }

        .pin-points li {
          font-size: 0.72rem;
          line-height: 1.35;
          padding-inline-start: 0.9rem;
        }
      }

      @media (max-width: 599px) and (max-height: 720px) {
        .pin-stage {
          gap: 0.55rem;
          padding-block: 0.35rem 0.65rem;
        }

        /* Shrunk from 58vw so the caption below has room without the phone
           and text together overflowing a short screen. */
        .pin-frame {
          width: min(44vw, 10rem);
        }

        .pin-callouts {
          min-height: 10.3rem;
        }

        .pin-step {
          font-size: 0.58rem;
        }

        .pin-feature {
          font-size: 1.3rem;
          margin-block: 0.05rem 0.2rem;
        }

        /* Henry asked for this back — it was hidden entirely to fit short
           screens, and the fix was to shrink and clamp it, not remove it. */
        .pin-caption {
          display: -webkit-box;
          -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
          -webkit-line-clamp: 1;
          overflow: hidden;
          font-size: 0.72rem;
          line-height: 1.3;
        }

        .pin-points,
        .pin-points li {
          font-size: 0.64rem;
        }

        .pin-points {
          gap: 0.12rem;
          margin-block-start: 0.3rem;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  /* Phones get the same pinned sequence as desktop when scroll timelines are
     available. Unsupported browsers and reduced-motion users still get the
     complete stacked layout above. */
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Pinned features",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "label", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "The app" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "title", "label": "Heading", "default": "It does the editing" },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "intro",
      "label": "Intro",
      "default": "<p>Free on the App Store, and it works on its own — you do not need the kit to use it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "tone",
      "label": "Background",
      "options": [
        { "value": "light", "label": "Base" },
        { "value": "dark", "label": "Raised" }
      ],
      "default": "dark"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The section pins to the viewport and the device turns through each screen as you scroll. Browsers without scroll-timeline support, and anyone who has asked for reduced motion, get a plain vertical list instead — the pinning is an enhancement, never the only way to read it."
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "scene",
      "name": "Scene",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Screenshot" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "asset_name", "label": "…or a theme asset filename", "info": "e.g. clippar-app-home.png in the theme's assets. Used only when no screenshot is picked above." },
        { "type": "text", "id": "alt", "label": "Alt text" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "feature", "label": "Feature label", "default": "Feature" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "caption", "label": "Caption", "default": "What this screen does." },
        {
          "type": "textarea",
          "id": "points",
          "label": "What it does",
          "info": "One per line. These are the individual features inside this part of the app — keep them short enough to read while scrolling past."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "max_blocks": 6,
  "presets": [{ "name": "Pinned features" }]
}
{% endschema %}
